have you ever looked around and wondered  where does all of my stuff come from  maybe you have a pencil that looks  something  like  this  now lots of different things go into  making a pencil but we can break it down  into a few basic parts  for example the wood that makes up the  body of the pencil comes from trees  most pencil erasers nowadays are made  from some kind of plastic which is  refined from oil and this aluminum ring  here at the base of the pencil and the  graphite tip at the top are both made  from different minerals  everything we use can be traced back to  natural  resources  which are things provided by the earth  and its processes  some natural resources that we use a lot  in our day-to-day lives are trees  fresh water  minerals  and fossil fuels like oil  we can divide these different types of  natural resources into renewable  resources  and  non-renewable resources  renewable resources can be replenished  in a relatively short amount of time  now i say relatively short because in  some cases it still might take quite a  few years for them to renew  for example if you have a tree  and you cut it down but you plan a new  tree to take its place  you're still going to have to wait let's  say 20 years before you can have another  fully grown tree  non-renewable resources are often very  limited and can take much longer than a  human lifetime for the earth to  replenish them  for example when carbon is compressed  for millions of years it can turn into a  diamond  once we mine that diamond out of the  earth it'll take millions of years for  another diamond to be formed that's the  same size  now just because a resource is renewable  doesn't mean we can use as much as we  want whenever we want  we know that trees are a renewable  resource right  however cutting down too many trees a  process called deforestation can have  downsides many animals rely on trees for  habitat or food and they can't exactly  wait 20 years for their homes to grow  back  plus taking wood out of the ecosystem  and burning it releases extra carbon  dioxide that's going to end up in the  atmosphere rather than naturally moving  through the carbon cycle this is one of  many things that contributes to climate  change  we also can't find all the natural  resources we need and all of the places  where we need them different places have  unique geographic or geologic features  like mountains and forests or rivers and  oceans  so they all have different resources  since natural resources aren't evenly  distributed different regions trade sell  or exchange their resources with each  other  for example let's say a community lives  at the base of these mountains here so  they can chop down wood from the forest  whenever they need it  if they go looking in the mountains they  might even find some rare minerals  another community down the road lives  near the river so they can go fishing  every day the soil near the river is  also really good for growing crops so  they farm a lot of tasty vegetables  now let's say the people in the mountain  community want to eat the fish pumpkins  and other crops harvested in the river  community  at the same time the river community  wants those hard to find minerals and  wood to make new farm tools  they'll need to find a way to share  their unique resources so everyone gets  what they need  now try and imagine this system on a  global scale  the things we rely on every day from the  pencils that you use to write to the  technologies that power our communities  they all connect us in some way back to  the resources that our planet naturally  provides  take a look around again  where in the world did the things in  your life really come from once you  break an object down to its original  pieces you might be surprised by what  you find
 

